    {
      "id": "ledger-notes",
      "kind": {
        "text": "THE DENSITY IS THE RESULT. That SOME binary16 value is lost by a trip through bfloat16 is obvious from the significand widths alone. What is established here is that 54784 of 65536 -- 83.59% -- are lost, so the round trip is not a rare-corner failure but the common case, and any pipeline that treats the two 16-bit formats as interchangeable buffers is wrong about five values in six.\n\nCONTRAST WITH ITS SIBLING. F:fp16-fp32-roundtrip-identity is the SAME shape of claim with binary32 in the middle, and it is `proved` with zero failures out of the same 65536. The two facts differ only in which 16-bit-or-wider format the value is staged through. bfloat16 has the WIDER exponent range of the two candidates -- it is not that it is a worse format, it is that range is not what a round trip needs.\n\nWHY THE FAILURE IS NOT ABOUT RANGE. bfloat16's exponent field is binary32's, so it contains binary16's range entirely: nothing here overflows to infinity or flushes to zero. Every one of the 54784 failures is pure precision loss in the significand, 11 bits down to 8. The reverse pairing would fail for the opposite reason.\n\nROUTE. `search-certificate`: settled by a replayable witness, matching the precedent of F:affirming-the-consequent, with the exhaustive enumeration supplying the density rather than the refutation.",
        "type": "prose"
      },
